@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+---
+type: adr
+status: stable
+entities: [User]
+related:
+  - resources/js/pages/auth/Login.tsx
+  - resources/js/pages/auth/Register.tsx
+  - app/Http/Controllers/Auth/LoginController.ts
+  - app/Http/Controllers/Auth/RegisterController.ts
+  - lang/**
+generated: { by: "claude-code/opus-5", at: 2026-08-18T09:51:13.659Z }
+---
+
+# Localize auth pages with per-locale catalogs
+
+## Context
+
+`/login` and `/register` are the first pages a new visitor sees, and
+their copy was written straight into the page components — English only.
+Japanese-speaking visitors (whose browsers send
+`Accept-Language: ja-JP,ja;q=0.9,…`) bounced there. The app already
+configured `createApp({ i18n })` with a single `lang/en/` catalog holding
+one welcome message, so the machinery existed but the pages did not use
+it.
+
+## Decision
+
+Auth-page copy lives in `lang/<locale>/auth.json`, nested one level per
+page (`auth.login.*`, `auth.register.*`). English and Japanese are the
+supported locales; `en` stays the fallback. Pages read their strings with
+`useTranslation()` from `@guren/inertia-client`, and the controllers set
+the document title with `this.t('auth.<page>.title')` so the
+server-rendered `<title>` matches the client-rendered `<Head>`.
+
+Locale detection is the framework default (`?locale=`, then a `locale`
+cookie, then `Accept-Language`, restricted to the supported list), so no
+custom middleware is involved: an unsupported or missing header resolves
+to `en`.
+
+**Every key must exist in every locale.** A key translated on one side
+only makes a page silently fall back to English for a single label, which
+reads worse than a fully English page. `bunx guren check` enforces key
+parity per locale; `tests/i18n.test.ts` additionally checks that each
+translation is actually translated and that every `:placeholder` in an
+English string survives into its counterpart.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Adding a locale is now a matter of dropping a `lang/<locale>/` directory
+next to the others and listing it in `src/app.ts` — no page changes. The
+cost is that new auth-page copy has to be added to both catalogs at once,
+or `guren check` fails. Pages outside `auth/` still carry hard-coded
+English; they can move into the catalog the same way when the need
+arises.
+
+Note that the shared `_i18n` Inertia prop ships the fallback (English)
+catalog alongside the request locale's, by framework design — so English
+strings appear in the page payload of a Japanese request even though
+nothing renders them.
